    {
      "question": "e-84",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "00695e667c23df4f50a5eda44ad8ada9f7807acb730c1cedab3ab89f7341f1ec",
      "new_sha256": "8de02b7575c8daddcbaab8bb3d27b684d7cb0c0274127fe0388bfc1e26bc7820",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-84",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Let's simplify the expression by factoring the denominator. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-{4}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(x+4)(x-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n(x+4)(x-4) = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=\\pm 4.\n$$\n\nConsequently, both $x=4$ and $x=-4$ are vertical asymptotes of $y=f(x).$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/example-84.json",
          "file_sha256": "eae785bca68b5b059f83ea3f302590a4931704c985d06254b1c56ec9de1f6646"
        }
      ],
