    {
      "question": "e-87",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "6bfb84d228ded5c09c953224481528195059a6608cf538656d0d81aefafd0440",
      "new_sha256": "4ed169fbcf25fe2a390dbb931e9757a6d171e6a0a878494ecaf75ece2863091f",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-87",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, let's put the two terms over a common denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{x+1} & =\\frac{(x+1)}{x(x+1)}+\\frac{x}{x(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1+x}{x(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{x(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we proceed to set the denominator equal to zero and get\n\n$$\nx(x+1) = 0\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=0,-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptotes are at $x=0$ and $x=-1.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979852/example-87.json",
          "file_sha256": "a7097274ae6b82300c3912b17dddeac75f773beee395f0347c605ca4461db83b"
        }
